helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\13020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154143				0	sk	Set left indent				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\13020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154307				0	sk	Drag the bottom left mark to the right while pressing the mouse button				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\13020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155449				0	sk	Set left indent of first line				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\13020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145673				0	sk	Drag the top left mark to the right while pressing the mouse button				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\13020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156156				0	sk	Set right indent				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\13020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153761				0	sk	Drag the mark on the right to the left while pressing the mouse button				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\13020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154760				0	sk	In order to change the left indent starting with the second line of a paragraph, hold down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> key, click the triangle on the bottom left, and drag it to the right.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\13020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148453				0	sk	Tabs that have been set are not changed when indenting a paragraph. If the set tabs end up outside the margins of the paragraph, they are no longer displayed, but they still exist.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	HTML Filters and Forms				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	bm_id3163829				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>forms; HTML filters\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163829				0	sk	HTML Filters and Forms				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3147285				0	sk	You can use all control elements and form events in HTML documents. There have been numerous events to date (for example, focus events), which have not been changed. They will continue to be imported and exported as ONFOCUS, ONBLUR, and so on for JavaScript and as SDONFOCUS, SDONBLUR, and so on for $[officename] Basic.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3150616				0	sk	Generic names that consist of the Listener interface and the method name of the event are used for all other events: An event registered as XListener::method is exported as				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3147571				0	sk	SDEvent-XListener-method = "/* event-code */"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3152425				0	sk	Note that the XListener- and method components of this option are case sensitive.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3153683				0	sk	Event handling of controls is performed using the $[officename] API. If you assign an event to a control, an object registers itself internally as a "Listener" for a specific control event. To do this, the object must use a specific interface, for example the XFocusListener Interface, so that it can react to focus events. When the event occurs, the control then invokes a special method of the Listener interface when the control receives the focus. The internally registered object then invokes the JavaScript or $[officename] Basic code, which was assigned to the event.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3156410				0	sk	The HTML filter now uses precisely these listener interfaces and method names so that it can import and export events as desired. You can register a focus event through				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3150506				0	sk	<INPUT TYPE=text ONFOCUS="/* code */"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154289				0	sk	rather than through the				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3155391				0	sk	<INPUT TYPE=text SDEvent-XFocusListener-focusGained="/* code */"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3152996				0	sk	register. Events can therefore be registered as desired, including those not offered in the list boxes. To define the script language of events, you can write the following line in the document header:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3150443				0	sk	<META HTTP-EQUIV="content-script-type" CONTENT="...">				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3166410				0	sk	As CONTENT you can, for example, use "text/x-StarBasic" for $[officename] Basic or a "text/JavaScript" for JavaScript. If no entry is made, JavaScript is assumed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\02\01170700.xhp	0	help	par_id3146797				0	sk	During exporting, the default script language will be defined based on the first module found in macro management. For events, only one language can be used per document.				20200411 15:09:53
