helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN105F9				0	sk	\<variable id=\"data_tables\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/data_tables.xhp\"\>Working with Tables\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN10617				0	sk	Data is stored in tables. As an example, your system address book that you use for your e-mail addresses is a table of the address book database. Each address is a data record, presented as a row in that table. The data records consist of data fields, for example the first and the last name fields and the e-mail field.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN1061A				0	sk	Creating a New Table With the Table Wizard				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN1061E				0	sk	In %PRODUCTNAME you can create a new table using the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/tablewizard00.xhp\"\>Table Wizard\</link\>:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN10632				0	sk	Open the database file where you want to create the new table.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN10636				0	sk	In the left pane of the database window, click the \<emph\>Tables\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN1063E				0	sk	Click \<emph\>Use Wizard to Create Table\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_tables.xhp	0	help	par_idN10645				0	sk	Creating a New Table With the Design View				20200411 15:09:53
