helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	Inserting Charts				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153910				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>charts; inserting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>plotting data as charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting; charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>spreadsheets; inserting charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>charts; editing data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing; chart data\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153910				0	sk	\<variable id=\"chart_insert\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/chart_insert.xhp\" name=\"Inserting Charts\"\>Inserting Charts\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3139133				0	sk	Different methods exist to start a chart:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id6772972				0	sk	Insert a chart based on data from cells in Calc or Writer.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id6049684				0	sk	These charts update automatically when the source data changes.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id2356944				0	sk	Insert a chart with a default data set, and then use the Data Table dialog to enter your own data for that chart.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id866115				0	sk	These charts can be created in Writer, Impress and Draw. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3146763				0	sk	Copy a chart from Calc or Writer into another document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id701315				0	sk	These charts are snapshots of the data at the time of copying. They do not change when the source data changes.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id4439832				0	sk	In Calc, a chart is an object on a sheet, it cannot be a sheet of its own.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id719931				0	sk	Chart in a Calc spreadsheet				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275				0	sk	Click inside the cell range that you want to present in your chart.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id7211218				0	sk	Click the \<emph\>Insert Chart\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> toolbar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id7549363				0	sk	You see a chart preview and the Chart Wizard.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id9091769				0	sk	Follow the instructions in the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart Wizard\</link\> to create the chart. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id3761406				0	sk	Chart in a Writer text document				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3155066	32			0	sk	In a Writer document, you can insert a chart based on the values in a Writer table. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id428479				0	sk	Click inside the Writer table. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id7236243				0	sk	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Object - Chart\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id6171452				0	sk	You see a chart preview and the Chart Wizard.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3145419	7			0	sk	Follow the instructions in the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart Wizard\</link\> to create the chart. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id6436658				0	sk	Chart based on values of its own				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id6944792				0	sk	In Writer, if you have not selected any cells, choose \<emph\>Insert - Object - Chart\</emph\> to insert a chart with default data. In Draw or Impress, choose \<emph\>Insert - Chart\</emph\> to insert a chart based on default data.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3152960				0	sk	You can change the default data values by double-clicking on the chart and then choosing \<link href=\"text/schart/01/03010000.xhp\" name=\"View - Chart Data Table\"\>\<emph\>View - Chart Data Table\</emph\>\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	Working with Forms				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	bm_id5762199				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>opening;forms\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>forms;creating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>design view;creating forms\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN105F9				0	sk	\<variable id=\"data_forms\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/data_forms.xhp\"\>Working with Forms\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10617				0	sk	Using forms, you can define how to present the data. Open a text document or a spreadsheet and insert the controls such as push buttons and list boxes. In the properties dialogs of the controls, you can define what data the forms should display.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1061A				0	sk	Creating a New Form With the Form Wizard				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1061E				0	sk	In %PRODUCTNAME, you can create a new form using the \<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01090000.xhp\"\>Form Wizard\</link\>:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10632				0	sk	Open the database file where you want to create the new form.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10636				0	sk	In the left pane of the database window, click the \<emph\>Forms\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1063E				0	sk	Click \<emph\>Use Wizard to Create Form\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10645				0	sk	Creating a New Form Manually				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1064C				0	sk	Open the database file where you want to create the new form.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10650				0	sk	In the left pane of the database window, click the \<emph\>Forms\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10658				0	sk	Click \<emph\>Create Form in Design View\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN1065F				0	sk	A new text document opens. Use the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170000.xhp\"\>Form Controls\</link\> to insert form controls.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_forms.xhp	0	help	par_idN10670				0	sk	Click the \<emph\>Forms\</emph\> icon to access all forms that were created from within the current database window. In addition, you can use the \<emph\>Form Controls\</emph\> icons to add database form controls to any Writer or Calc document, but these documents will not be listed in the database window. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	About Digital Signatures				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	bm_id7430951				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>certificates\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>digital signatures;overview\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>security;digital signatures\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id2767418				0	sk	\<variable id=\"digital_signatures\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/digital_signatures.xhp\"\>About Digital Signatures\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10632				0	sk	In %PRODUCTNAME, you can digitally sign your documents and macros. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id6564531				0	sk	Certificates				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10639				0	sk	To sign a document digitally, you need a personal key, the certificate. A personal key is stored on your computer as a combination of a private key, which must be kept secret, and a public key, which you add to your documents when you sign them.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066D				0	sk	Save and sign the document				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10671				0	sk	When you apply a digital signature to a document, a kind of checksum is computed from the document's content plus your personal key. The checksum and your public key are stored together with the document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10674				0	sk	Open a signed document				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN10678				0	sk	When someone later opens the document on any computer with a recent version of %PRODUCTNAME, the program will compute the checksum again and compare it with the stored checksum. If both are the same, the program will signal that you see the original, unchanged document. In addition, the program can show you the public key information from the certificate.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067B				0	sk	You can compare the public key with the public key that is published on the web site of the certificate authority.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067E				0	sk	Whenever someone changes something in the document, this change breaks the digital signature. After the change, there will be no sign that you see the original document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2008200911381426				0	sk	The result of the signature validation is displayed in the status bar and within the Digital Signature dialog. Several documents and macro signatures can exist inside an ODF document. If there is a problem with one signature, then the validation result of that one signature is assumed for all signatures. That is, if there are ten valid signatures and one invalid signature, then the status bar and the status field in the dialog will flag the signature as invalid.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200911571878				0	sk	You can see any of the following icons and messages when you open a signed document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504050				0	sk	Icon in Status bar				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504061				0	sk	Signature status				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504010				0	sk	\<image id=\"img_id0821200912421569\" src=\"xmlsecurity/res/certificate_16.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1665in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id0821200912421569\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504189				0	sk	The signature is valid.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id082120091250418				0	sk	\<image id=\"img_id0821200912431081\" src=\"xmlsecurity/res/notcertificate_16.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1665in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id0821200912431081\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504133				0	sk	The signature is OK, but the certificates could not be validated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504165				0	sk	The signature and the certificate are OK, but not all parts of the document are signed. (For documents that were signed with old versions of the software, see note below.)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504237				0	sk	\<image id=\"img_id0821200912435090\" src=\"xmlsecurity/res/caution_11x16.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1146in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id0821200912435090\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200912504233				0	sk	The signature is invalid.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id0821200910191787				0	sk	Signatures and software versions				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200910191747				0	sk	The signing of contents got changed with OpenOffice.org 3.2 and StarOffice 9.2. Now all contents of the files, except the signature file itself (META-INF/documentsignatures.xml) are signed. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id0821200910191774				0	sk	When you sign a document with OpenOffice.org 3.2 or StarOffice 9.2 or a later version, and you open that document in an older version of the software, the signature will be displayed as "invalid". Signatures created with older versions of the software will be marked with "only parts of the documents are signed" when loaded in the newer software.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2008200911583098				0	sk	When you load an ODF document, you might see an icon in the status bar and the status field in the dialog that indicates that the document is only partially signed. This status will appear when the signature and certificate are valid, but they were created with a version of OpenOffice.org before 3.2 or StarOffice before 9.2. In versions of OpenOffice.org before 3.0 or StarOffice before 9.0, the document signature was applied to the main contents, pictures and embedded objects only and some contents, like macros, were not signed. In OpenOffice.org 3.0 and StarOffice 9.0 the document signature was applied to most content, including macros. However, the mimetype and the content of the META-INF folder were not signed. And in OpenOffice.org 3.2 and StarOffice 9.2 all contents, except the signature file itself (META-INF/documentsignatures.xml), are signed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	hd_id9354228				0	sk	Security Warnings				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id2372508				0	sk	When you receive a signed document, and the software reports that the signature is valid, this does not mean that you can be absolutely sure that the document is the same that the sender has sent. Signing documents with software certificates is not a perfectly secure method. Numerous ways are possible to circumvent the security features.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id7953123				0	sk	Example: Think about someone who wants to camouflage his identity to be a sender from your bank. He can easily get a certificate using a false name, then send you any signed e-mail pretending he is working for your bank. You will get that e-mail, and the e-mail or the document within has the "valid signed" icon. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id6195257				0	sk	Do not trust the icon. Inspect and verify the certificates.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id8635517				0	sk	The validation of a signature is not a legally binding guarantee of any kind.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id6075624				0	sk	On Windows operating systems, the Windows features of validating a signature are used. On Solaris and Linux systems, files that are supplied by Thunderbird, Mozilla or Firefox are used. You must ensure that the files that are in use within your system are really the original files that were supplied by the original developers. For malevolent intruders, there are numerous ways to replace original files with other files that they supply.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id6819971				0	sk	The messages about validation of a signature that you see in %PRODUCTNAME are the messages that the validation files return. The %PRODUCTNAME software has no way to ensure that the messages reflect the true status of any certificate. The %PRODUCTNAME software only displays the messages that other files that are not under control of %PRODUCTNAME report. There is no legal responsibility of %PRODUCTNAME that the displayed messages reflect the true status of a digital signature.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\digital_signatures.xhp	0	help	par_id3204443				0	sk	\<link href=\"https://wiki.openoffice.org/wiki/How_to_use_digital_Signatures\"\>English Wiki page on digital signatures\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
