helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3145068				0	sk	Choose \<emph\>File - Save As\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3153379				0	sk	In the \<emph\>File type\</emph\> box, select a Microsoft Office file format.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154138				0	sk	Saving Documents by Default in Microsoft Office Formats				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3144760				0	sk	Choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01010200.xhp\" name=\"Load/Save - General\"\>Load/Save - General\</link\>\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3148453				0	sk	In the \<emph\>Default file format and ODF settings\</emph\> area, first select a document type, then select the file type for saving.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3149807	18			0	sk	From now on, if you save a document, the \<emph\>File type \</emph\>will be set according to your choice. Of course, you still can select another file type in the file save dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156423				0	sk	Opening Microsoft Office Files by Default				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153092				0	sk	Converting Many Microsoft Office Files into OpenDocument Format				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3146986				0	sk	The \<emph\>Document Converter Wizard\</emph\> will copy and convert all Microsoft Office files in a folder into $[officename] documents in the OpenDocument file format. You can specify the folder to be read, and the folder where the converted files are to be saved.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3150486				0	sk	Choose \<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01130000.xhp\" name=\"File - AutoPilot - Document Converter\"\>\<emph\>File - Wizards - Document Converter\</emph\>\</link\> to start the wizard.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154319				0	sk	Macros in Microsoft Office and $[officename]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3154921				0	sk	With a few exceptions, Microsoft Office and $[officename] cannot run the same macro code. Microsoft Office uses VBA (Visual Basic for Applications) code, and $[officename] uses Basic code based on the $[officename] API (Application Program Interface) environment. Although the programming language is the same, the objects and methods are different.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id0804200804173539				0	sk	The most recent versions of %PRODUCTNAME can run some Excel Visual Basic scripts if you enable this feature at \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Load/Save - VBA Properties\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	sk	If you use macros in one of the applications and want to use the same functionality in the other application, you must edit the macros. $[officename] can load the macros that are contained within Microsoft Office files and you can then view and edit the macro code in the $[officename] \<link href=\"text/shared/main0600.xhp\" name=\"Basic IDE\"\>Basic IDE\</link\> editor.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152596				0	sk	You can choose to preserve or delete VBA macros				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3153144				0	sk	Open a Microsoft Office document that contains VBA macro code. Change only the normal contents (text, cells, graphics), and do not edit the macros. Save the document as a Microsoft Office file type. Open the file in Microsoft Office, and the VBA macros will run as before.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3150011				0	sk	You may delete the VBA macros from the Microsoft Office file on loading or on saving.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\ms_user.xhp	0	help	par_id3155366				0	sk	Choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01130100.xhp\" name=\"Load/Save - VBA Properties\"\>Load/Save - VBA Properties\</link\>\</emph\> to set the VBA macro handling of $[officename].				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\filternavigator.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	Using the Filter Navigator				20200411 15:09:53
