helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN105FC				0	sk	\<variable id=\"jdbc\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/dabawiz02jdbc.xhp\"\>JDBC Connection\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10600				0	sk	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Specifies the options to access a \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000005.xhp#jdbc\" name=\"JDBC\"\>JDBC\</link\> database.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10623				0	sk	JDBC Examples				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10627				0	sk	\<item type=\"productname\"\>You can use a JDBC driver class to connect to a JDBC database from %PRODUCTNAME\</item\>. The driver class is provided by the database manufacturer. Two examples of JDBC databases are Oracle and MySQL.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1062D				0	sk	The driver classes must be added to %PRODUCTNAME in \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME - Java.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10634				0	sk	Oracle database				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10638				0	sk	You can use a JDBC driver to access an Oracle database from Solaris or Linux. To access the database from Windows, you need an ODBC driver.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1064B				0	sk	On UNIX, ensure that the Oracle database client is installed with JDBC support. The JDBC driver class for the Solaris Oracle client version 8.x is located in the <Oracle client>/product/jdbc/lib/classes111.zip directory. You can also download the latest version from the Oracle web site:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1064E				0	sk	http://otn.oracle.com/tech/java/sqlj_jdbc/content.html				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10661				0	sk	In the \<emph\>Data source URL\</emph\> box, enter the location of the Oracle database server. The syntax of the URL depends on the database type. See the documentation that came with the JDBC driver for more information. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10668				0	sk	For an Oracle database, the syntax of the URL is:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066B				0	sk	oracle:thin:@hostname:port:database_name				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10674				0	sk	hostname is the name of the machine that runs the Oracle database. You can also replace hostname with the IP address of the server.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10678				0	sk	port is the port where the Oracle database listens. Ask your database administrator for the correct port address.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067C				0	sk	database_name is the name of the Oracle database. Ask your database administrator for the correct name.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1067F				0	sk	MySQL database				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10683				0	sk	The driver for the MySQL database is available on the MySQL web site.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10689				0	sk	The syntax for a MySQL database is:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1068C				0	sk	mysql://hostname:port/database_name				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10695				0	sk	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>hostname is the name of the machine that runs the MySQL database.\</ahelp\> You can also replace hostname with the IP address of the server.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN10699				0	sk	port is the default port for MySQL databases, namely 3306.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN1069D				0	sk	database_name is the name of the database.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A0				0	sk	Data source URL				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A4				0	sk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_CONNURL_JDBCPAGE\"\>Enter the URL for the database. For example, for the MySQL JDBC driver, enter "jdbc:mysql://<Servername>/<name of the database>". For more information on the JDBC driver, consult the documentation that came with the driver.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN106BB				0	sk	JDBC Driver Class				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN106BF				0	sk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_DRIVERCLASS\"\>Enter the name of the JDBC driver.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_id7953733				0	sk	Before you can use a JDBC driver, you need to add its class path. Choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME\</emph\>\<emph\>- Java\</emph\>, and click the \<emph\>Class Path\</emph\> button. After you add the path information, restart \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CE				0	sk	Test class				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\dabawiz02jdbc.xhp	0	help	par_idN106E4				0	sk	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Tests the connection with the current settings.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
