helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\05010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155186				0	sk	\<ahelp hid=\"STARMATH:PUSHBUTTON:RID_FONTTYPEDIALOG:2\"\>Click this button to save your changes as the default for all new formulas.\</ahelp\> After confirming the changes, click the \<emph\>Yes\</emph\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090906.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	Matica v tučným typom písma				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090906.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154704				0	sk	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090906.xhp\" name=\"Matrix in Bold Font\"\>Matrix in Bold Font\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090906.xhp	0	help	par_id3150342				0	sk	Here is an example of how to create a bold font matrix in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>. You can copy this example to the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window using the clipboard and use it in your own formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090906.xhp	0	help	par_id3148866				0	sk	\<image id=\"img_id3150210\" src=\"res/helpimg/smzb6.png\" width=\"9.511cm\" height=\"7.99cm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150210\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090906.xhp	0	help	par_id3154763				0	sk	bold { f(x", "y) = left [ stack { x + y over z + left lbrace matrix { 2 # 3 # 4 ## 4 # 5 # 6 ## 6 # 7 # 8} right rbrace # {y + sin (x)} over %alpha # z + y over g } right ]}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	Atribúty				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148839				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>attributes; additional information\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148839				0	sk	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091300.xhp\" name=\"Attributes\"\>Attributes\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150051				0	sk	Additional information about attributes in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> is found here.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146966				0	sk	The \<emph\>acute\</emph\>, \<emph\>bar\</emph\>, \<emph\>breve\</emph\>, \<emph\>check\</emph\>, \<emph\>circle\</emph\>, \<emph\>dot\</emph\>, \<emph\>ddot\</emph\>, \<emph\>dddot\</emph\>, \<emph\>grave\</emph\>, \<emph\>hat\</emph\>, \<emph\>tilde\</emph\> and \<emph\>vec\</emph\> attributes always have a fixed size and do not become wider (longer) if they are above a long symbol. By default, the attributes are centered.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154557				0	sk	The only attributes which grow with the length of the symbol are \<emph\>overline\</emph\>, \<emph\>underline\</emph\> and \<emph\>overstrike\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151180				0	sk	For some character strings, it is possible that a line inserted with \<emph\>underline\</emph\> is too close to the character. In this case, an empty group can solve the problem: \<emph\>underline Q sub {}\</emph\> instead of \<emph\>underline Q\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
