helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3004547				0	sk	The open, low, high, and closing values of a row build together one data unit in the chart. A stock price data series consists of several rows containing such data units. The column containing the transaction volume builds an optional second data series.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id6401867				0	sk	Depending on the chosen variant, you do not need all columns.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id18616				0	sk	Stock Chart Variants				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id6138492				0	sk	Choose the \<emph\>Stock\</emph\> chart type on the first page of the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart wizard\</link\>. Then select one of the four variants.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id4569231				0	sk	Typ 1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id291451				0	sk	Based on\<emph\> low\</emph\>         \<emph\>and high \</emph\>column the Type 1 shows the distance between bottom price (low) and top price (high) by a vertical line.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3341776				0	sk	Based on\<emph\> low, high,\</emph\> and \<emph\>close\</emph\> column Type 1 shows an additional horizontal mark \<emph\>for\</emph\> the closing price.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id5947141				0	sk	Typ 2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id1911679				0	sk	Based on \<emph\>open, low, high\</emph\>, and \<emph\>close\</emph\> column Type 2 generates the traditional "candle stick" chart. Type 2 draws the vertical line between the bottom and top price and adds a rectangle in front, which visualizes the range between the opening and closing price. If you click on the rectangle you see more information in the status bar. %PRODUCTNAME uses different fill colors for rising values (the opening price is lower than the closing price) and falling values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id9364909				0	sk	Typ 3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id4473403				0	sk	Based on \<emph\>volume, low, high\</emph\>, and \<emph\>close\</emph\> column chart Type 3 draws a chart like Type 1, with additional columns for the transaction volume.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id4313791				0	sk	Typ 4				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id4331797				0	sk	Based on all five data columns \<emph\>volume, open, low, high\</emph\>, and \<emph\>close\</emph\>, Type 4 combines a chart of Type 2 with a column chart for the transaction volume.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id4191717				0	sk	Because measurement for transaction volume might be "units", a second y axis is introduced in chart Type 3 and Type 4. The price axis is shown on the right side and the volume axis on the left side.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id2318796				0	sk	Setting the Data Source				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id399182				0	sk	Charts based on its own data				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id5298318				0	sk	To change the data series of a chart having its own data, choose \<link href=\"text/schart/01/03010000.xhp\"\>Chart Data Table\</link\> from the View menu or from the context menu of the chart in edit mode.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7588732				0	sk	In an embedded chart data table, the data series are always organized in columns.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id95828				0	sk	For a new stock chart first use a column chart. Add the columns you need and enter your data in the order which is shown in the example, omitting any columns not required for the desired variant. Use Move Series Right to change the column order. Close the chart data table. Now use the Chart Type dialog to change to the stock chart variant. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id6182744				0	sk	If you have already got a stock chart and you want to change the variant, then first change the chart type to a column chart, add or remove columns so that it fits to the variant, and then change the chart type back to a stock chart.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3496200				0	sk	Do not write the name of a data series in a row. Write the name into the field above the role name.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7599108				0	sk	The order of the rows determines how the categories are arranged in the chart. Use Move Row Down to change the order.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id888698				0	sk	Charts based on Calc or Writer tables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3394573				0	sk	You can choose or alter a data range on the second page of the Chart wizard or in the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_data_range.xhp\"\>Data Range\</link\> dialog. For fine tuning use the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_data_series.xhp\"\>Data Series\</link\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7594225				0	sk	To specify a data range do one of the following:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id5081637				0	sk	Enter the data range in the text box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id9759514				0	sk	In Calc, an example data range would be "$Sheet1.$B$3:$B$14". Note that a data range may consist of more than one region in a spreadsheet, e.g. "$Sheet1.A1:A5;$Sheet1.D1:D5" is also a valid data range. In Writer, an example data range would be "Table1.A1:E4".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id1614429				0	sk	As long as the syntax is not correct, %PRODUCTNAME shows the text in red.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id1589098				0	sk	In Calc, click \<emph\>Select data range\</emph\> to minimize the dialog, then drag to select the data range. When you release the mouse, the data are entered. Click \<emph\>Select \</emph\>               \<emph\>data range\</emph\> again to add a data range. In the input field of the minimized dialog, click after the entry and type a semicolon. Then drag to select the next range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id8746910				0	sk	Click one of the options for data series in rows or in columns. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id9636524				0	sk	Your stock chart data are "in columns", if the information in a row belongs to the same "candle stick".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id5675527				0	sk	Fine Tuning the Data Ranges of Table Based Stock Charts				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3486434				0	sk	You can organize data series and edit the source for parts of single data series on the third page of the Chart wizard or on the page Data Series in the Data Range dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id3068636				0	sk	Organize Data Series				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2480849				0	sk	In the \<emph\>data series\</emph\> area on the left side of the dialog, you can organize the data series of the actual chart. A stock chart has at least one data series containing the prices. It might have a second data series for transaction volume.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id4181951				0	sk	If you have got more than one price data series, use the Up and Down arrow buttons to order them. The order determines the arrangement in the chart. Do the same for volume data series. You cannot switch price and volume data series.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2927335				0	sk	To remove a data series, select the data series in the list and click \<emph\>Remove\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2107303				0	sk	To add a data series, select one of the existing data series and click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>. You get an empty entry below the selected one, which has the same type. If you have no price data series or no volume data series, you must first select a range for these series in the \<emph\>Data Range\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id4071779				0	sk	Setting Data Ranges				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7844477				0	sk	In the \<emph\>Data Ranges\</emph\> dialog you can set or change the data range of each component of the selected data series.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id6478469				0	sk	In the upper list you see the role name of the components and the current values. When you have selected a role, you can change the value in the text box below the list. The label shows the selected role.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id9038972				0	sk	Enter the range into the text box or click on \<emph\>Select data range\</emph\> to minimize the dialog and select the range with the mouse.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7985168				0	sk	Select Open Values, Close Values, High Values, and Low Values in any order. Specify only the ranges for those roles which you need for the chosen variant of the stock chart. The ranges need not be next to each other in the table.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id876186				0	sk	Legenda				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3939634				0	sk	The legend displays the labels from the first row or column or from the special range that you have set in the \<emph\>Data Series\</emph\> dialog. If your chart does not contain labels, the legend displays text like "Row 1, Row 2, ...", or "Column A, Column B, ..." according to the row number or column letter of the chart data.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2377697				0	sk	The legend shows the value from the range, which you entered in the \<emph\>Range for Name\</emph\> field in the \<emph\>Data Range\</emph\> dialog. The default entry is the column header of the closing price column.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2188787				0	sk	Select one of the position options. When the chart is finished, you can specify other positions using the Format menu.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05060000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	Bok grafu				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05060000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150792				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>charts; formatting walls\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;chart walls\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05060000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150792				0	sk	Bok grafu				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05060000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154685	2			0	sk	\<variable id=\"diagramm\"\>\<ahelp visibility=\"visible\" hid=\".uno:DiagramWall\"\>Opens the\<emph\> Chart Wall\</emph\> dialog, where you can modify the properties of the chart wall. The chart wall is the "vertical" background behind the data area of the chart.\</ahelp\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_bubble.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	Chart Type Bubble				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_bubble.xhp	0	help	bm_id2183975				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>bubble charts\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>chart types;bubble\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_bubble.xhp	0	help	hd_id1970722				0	sk	\<variable id=\"type_bubble\"\>\<link href=\"text/schart/01/type_bubble.xhp\"\>Chart Type Bubble\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_bubble.xhp	0	help	par_id40589				0	sk	On the first page of the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart Wizard\</link\> you can choose a chart type.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_bubble.xhp	0	help	hd_id0526200904491284				0	sk	Bubble				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_bubble.xhp	0	help	par_id0526200904491222				0	sk	A bubble chart shows the relations of three variables. Two variables are used for the position on the X-axis and Y-axis, while the third variable is shown as the relative size of each bubble.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_bubble.xhp	0	help	par_id0526200906040162				0	sk	The data series dialog for a bubble chart has an entry to define the data range for the Bubble Sizes.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	Chart Type XY				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	bm_id84231				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>scatter charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>XY charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>chart types;XY (scatter)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>error indicators in charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>error bars in charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>averages in charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>statistics in charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variances in charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>standard deviation in charts\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	hd_id9346598				0	sk	\<variable id=\"type_xy\"\>\<link href=\"text/schart/01/type_xy.xhp\"\>Chart Type XY (Scatter)\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id2003845				0	sk	On the first page of the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart Wizard\</link\> you can choose a chart type. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	hd_id7757194				0	sk	XY (Bodový rozptyl)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id5977965				0	sk	An XY chart in its basic form is based on one data series consisting of a name, a list of x‑values, and a list of y‑values. Each value pair (x|y) is shown as a point in a coordinate system. The name of the data series is associated with the y‑values and shown in the legend.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id4381847				0	sk	Choose an XY chart for the following example tasks:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id1336710				0	sk	scale the x‑axis				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id1221655				0	sk	generate a parameter curve, for example a spiral				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id3397320				0	sk	draw the graph of a function				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id7657399				0	sk	explore the statistical association of quantitative variables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id8925138				0	sk	Your XY chart may have more than one data series.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	hd_id5461897				0	sk	XY Chart Variants				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id8919339				0	sk	You can choose an XY chart variant on the first page of the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart Wizard\</link\>, or by choosing \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Chart Type \</item\>for a chart in edit mode.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id4634235				0	sk	The chart is created with default settings. After the chart is finished, you can edit its properties to change the appearance. Line styles and icons can be changed on the \<emph\>Line\</emph\>tab page of the data series properties dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id5482039				0	sk	Double-click any data point to open the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data Series\</item\> dialog. In this dialog, you can change many properties of the data series.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id0805200810492449				0	sk	For 2D charts, you can choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Y Error Bars\</item\> to enable the display of error bars.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id6221198				0	sk	You can enable the display of mean value lines and trend lines using commands on the Insert menu.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	hd_id1393475				0	sk	Points only				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id6571550				0	sk	Each data point is shown by an icon. %PRODUCTNAME uses default icons with different forms and colors for each data series. The default colors are set in \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Charts - Default Colors\</item\>. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	hd_id5376140				0	sk	Iba čiary				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id4408093				0	sk	This variant draws straight lines from one data point to the next. The data points are not shown by icons. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id7261268				0	sk	The drawing order is the same as the order in the data series. Mark \<emph\>Sort by X Values\</emph\> to draw the lines in the order of the x values. This sorting applies only to the chart, not to the data in the table.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	hd_id6949369				0	sk	Body a čiary				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id9611499				0	sk	This variant shows points and lines at the same time.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	hd_id6765953				0	sk	3D Čiary				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id7422711				0	sk	The lines are shown like tapes. The data points are not shown by icons. In the finished chart choose \<link href=\"text/schart/01/three_d_view.xhp\"\>3D View\</link\> to set properties like illumination and angle of view.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	hd_id239265				0	sk	Plynulé čiary				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id7957396				0	sk	Mark \<emph\>Smooth Lines\</emph\> to draw curves instead of straight line segments.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id1202124				0	sk	Click \<emph\>Properties\</emph\> to set details for the curves.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id5989562				0	sk	\<emph\>Cubic Spline\</emph\> interpolates your data points with polynomials of degree 3. The transitions between the polynomial pieces are smooth, having the same slope and curvature. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id6128421				0	sk	The \<emph\>Resolution\</emph\> determines how many line segments are calculated to draw a piece of polynomial between two data points. You can see the intermediate points if you click any data point.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_xy.xhp	0	help	par_id9280373				0	sk	\<emph\>B-Spline\</emph\> uses the data points as control points to form a B-spline curve. Those curves are built piecewise from polynomials. The \<emph\>Data points order\</emph\> sets the degree k of these polynomials. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05010000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	sk	Format Selection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05010000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149666				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>objects;properties of charts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>charts; properties\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>properties;charts\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05010000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149666				0	sk	Format Selection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	sk	\<variable id=\"objekteigenschaften\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Formats the selected object.\</ahelp\>\</variable\> Depending on the object selected, the command opens dialogs that you can also open by choosing the following commands from the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> menu:				20200411 15:09:53
