helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3158426				0	sk	What is the effective annual rate of interest for a 5.25% nominal rate and quarterly payment.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148927	422			0	sk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=EFFECT_ADD(0.0525;4)\</item\> returns 0.053543 or 5.3543%.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149998				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; arithmetic-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>arithmetic-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>depreciations;arithmetic-degressive\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>DDB function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149998				0	sk	DDB				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159190				0	sk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GDA\"\>Returns the depreciation of an asset for a specified period using the arithmetic-declining method.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152361				0	sk	Use this form of depreciation if you require a higher initial depreciation value as opposed to linear depreciation. The depreciation value gets less with each period and is usually used for assets whose value loss is higher shortly after purchase (for example, vehicles, computers). Please note that the book value will never reach zero under this calculation type.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156038				0	sk	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3166452				0	sk	DDB(Cost; Salvage; Life; Period; Factor)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153237	104			0	sk	            \<emph\>Cost\</emph\> fixes the initial cost of an asset.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149787	105			0	sk	            \<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> fixes the value of an asset at the end of its life.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152945	106			0	sk	            \<emph\>Life\</emph\> is the number of periods (for example, years or months) defining how long the asset is to be used.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149736	107			0	sk	            \<emph\>Period\</emph\> states the period for which the value is to be calculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150243	108			0	sk	            \<emph\>Factor\</emph\> (optional) is the factor by which depreciation decreases. If a value is not entered, the default is factor 2.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159274				0	sk	Príklad				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152882				0	sk	A computer system with an initial cost of 75,000 currency units is to be depreciated monthly over 5 years. The value at the end of the depreciation is to be 1 currency unit. The factor is 2.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154106	111			0	sk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=DDB(75000;1;60;12;2) \</item\>= 1,721.81 currency units. Therefore, the double-declining depreciation in the twelfth month after purchase is 1,721.81 currency units.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149962				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; geometric-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>geometric-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>depreciations;geometric-degressive\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>DB function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149962				0	sk	DB				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148989				0	sk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GDA2\"\>Returns the depreciation of an asset for a specified period using the double-declining balance method.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156213				0	sk	This form of depreciation is used if you want to get a higher depreciation value at the beginning of the depreciation (as opposed to linear depreciation). The depreciation value is reduced with every depreciation period by the depreciation already deducted from the initial cost.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149807				0	sk	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153349				0	sk	DB(Cost; Salvage; Life; Period; Month)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148462	118			0	sk	            \<emph\>Cost\</emph\> is the initial cost of an asset.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148658	119			0	sk	            \<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> is the value of an asset at the end of the depreciation.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145371	120			0	sk	            \<emph\>Life\</emph\> defines the period over which an asset is depreciated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154608	121			0	sk	            \<emph\>Period\</emph\> is the length of each period. The length must be entered in the same date unit as the depreciation period.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150829	122			0	sk	            \<emph\>Month\</emph\> (optional) denotes the number of months for the first year of depreciation. If an entry is not defined, 12 is used as the default.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151130				0	sk	Príklad				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156147				0	sk	A computer system with an initial cost of 25,000 currency units is to be depreciated over a three year period. The salvage value is to be 1,000 currency units. One period is 30 days.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149513	125			0	sk	            \<item type=\"input\"\>=DB(25000;1000;36;1;6)\</item\> = 1,075.00 currency units				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159242				0	sk	The fixed-declining depreciation of the computer system is 1,075.00 currency units.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153948				0	sk	\<bookmark_value\>IRR function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>calculating;internal rates of return, regular payments\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>internal rates of return;regular payments\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153948				0	sk	IRR				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3143282				0	sk	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_IKV\"\>Calculates the internal rate of return for an investment.\</ahelp\> The values represent cash flow values at regular intervals, at least one value must be negative (payments), and at least one value must be positive (income).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150599				0	sk	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
